  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every finite-dimensional real inner-product space E equipped with its Borel measurable structure, every Borel probability measure mu on E, and real b,a satisfying b>=0,a>0, there exist Markov kernels T,H:E to E and B:E times E to E times E. For every u, T(u) is mu reweighted by exp(-(b/2)*norm(x-u)^2) and normalized by its integral against mu. For every u, H(u) is the law of X+sqrt(a)*Z, with X of law T(u) and independent centered identity-covariance Gaussian Z. Noise standard-deviation scale is sqrt(a), covariance aI. For every u,y, let Q(u,y) be T(u) reweighted by exp(-norm(x-y)^2/(2a)) and normalized by its integral against T(u). B(u,y) is the pushforward of Q(u,y) by x to (u,x), retaining u. The same B for every u,y also equals the pushforward by x to (u,x) of mu reweighted by exp(-(c/2)*norm(x-m(u,y))^2) and normalized by its mu integral, where c=b+a^-1 and m(u,y)=c^-1*(b*u+a^-1*y). Finally for every Borel probability nu on E, B composed with nu compProd H equals nu compProd T as actual measures on E times E. Explicitly sample U from nu, Y conditionally from H(U), then a pair from B(U,Y); its law equals sampling U from nu and X conditionally from T(U). This is equality of the full joint law of retained center and final sample, not merely sample marginal equality, and does not assert independence of center and sample. Quantifier order is all E,mu,b,a satisfying hypotheses, then exists T,H,B, then all probability nu. The same kernels precede and do not depend on nu. All fiber identities hold everywhere, not merely almost everywhere. Parameter measurability is included in the conclusion through kernel types, and probability of every fiber through Markov assertions. No separately supplied family measurability, density hypothesis on mu, moment, smoothness or convexity condition is assumed. The only numerical restrictions are b>=0 and a>0, with no unspecified constants. Reconstructed only from the anonymous statement and approved definitions; no source-fidelity or compilation verdict.",
